 {"key": "r-groups", "label": "verb groups", "kind": "rule", "chunk": "How verbs work", "table": "dict", "brief": "The three verb groups: ichidan (-iru/-eru, drop ru), godan (change the last syllable), irregular suru and kuru; the -ru godan lookalikes (kaeru go home, hairu, shiru, hashiru, iru need, kiru cut)."},
 {"key": "r-stems", "label": "the four stems", "kind": "rule", "chunk": "How verbs work", "table": "masu", "brief": "Godan verbs move the last vowel: a-row before -nai, i-row before -masu/-tai, e-row for -ba/potential/command, o-row for -ou; ichidan just drops ru."},
 {"key": "r-te", "label": "-te / -ta sound changes", "kind": "rule", "chunk": "How verbs work", "table": "te", "brief": "u/tsu/ru -> tte, mu/bu/nu -> nde, ku -> ite, gu -> ide, su -> shite; iku -> itte; -ta follows the same changes."},
 {"key": "r-irregular", "label": "the exceptions", "kind": "rule", "chunk": "How verbs work", "table": "nai", "brief": "suru (shinai, shite, dekiru), kuru (konai, kite, korareru), iku (itte), aru (nai), ii (yoku, yokatta), irassharu/kudasaru/ossharu/nasaru (-aimasu)."},
